# Privacy Policy

Last updated: 22 November 2026

StreamSetu is a player. It does not collect, sell or share your personal information, and the people who make it receive nothing from your use of it except a playback report when a stream fails, and only if you choose to share it: nothing is sent automatically unless you turn that on, and you can also send one by hand (described below). There are no analytics, no advertising identifiers, no automatic crash reporting and no account. Like any app that uses the internet, it contacts the services listed below, which see your internet address.

## What stays on your device

- The playlists and Xtream accounts you add, and the channels, movies, series and guide they contain.
- Your favourites, lists, watch history, resume positions and settings.
- Your playlist logins, kept in the system Keychain (or, on a Mac build without Keychain access, an encrypted file on your Mac).
- A short playback log: which server a stream came from (its name only), which player handled it, timings and errors. It never holds logins or full stream addresses and is trimmed automatically. On iPhone and iPad it never leaves your device. In the Mac app it leaves your device when a stream fails for good (an automatic report, only if you said yes when the app first asked or turned it on in Settings → Send Feedback), when you press Send My Log Now, or when you send feedback with diagnostics. You can clear it in Send Feedback (Mac).
- Backups you create yourself. They are files you choose where to keep.

## Who your device talks to

StreamSetu connects only to the places below, and only for the reason given.

- **Your playlist provider and your own servers.** The servers you add: an Xtream, M3U, XMLTV or Stalker / MAG portal provider, a Plex, Jellyfin or Emby media server, a TV tuner such as HDHomeRun or Tvheadend, a podcast feed, or a file share. StreamSetu contacts them to read your playlist, your guide and your library and to play streams or files, using the login you typed, from the moment you add them until you remove them. What such a server sees (your address, what you watch) is governed by whoever runs it. StreamSetu has no control over it. The only way to stop it is to remove the source.
- **StreamSetu's log service (Mac app only, off until you turn it on).** The iPhone and iPad app does not send logs. In the Mac app, only if you turn it on in Settings → Send Feedback (the app never asks on its own), then when a stream fails for good the recent playback log is sent; it is also sent when you press Send My Log Now, even with the setting off. The playback log is sent to a server run by the developer, hosted by Supabase in London (United Kingdom). It contains which player was used, timings and error codes, your app version, system version and device model, and a random report number made on your device that groups one device's reports (it is not linked to any account). Server names in the log are replaced with server-1, server-2 and so on. It never contains your logins, passwords, playlist names, titles or stream addresses. It is used only to fix playback problems, is not shared with anyone else, and is deleted after 30 days. You can see exactly what would be sent, including the report number, under See exactly what is sent in Send Feedback. To have a report deleted sooner, email contact@lokeshreddy.dev with that number.
- **GitHub (update checks and the official add-on list).** The Mac download checks for new versions about once a day through Sparkle, by reading a public file (appcast.xml) kept on GitHub (raw.githubusercontent.com). The Android app, when its update check is switched on, asks api.github.com for the public list of releases and, if you accept an update, downloads it from GitHub; it also reads and verifies a signed list of official add-ons from raw.githubusercontent.com. The Windows app reads the release list from api.github.com and downloads updates from github.com. These requests carry no account and no identifier, only the app's version, as any web client does; GitHub sees your internet address. The iPhone and iPad app and store builds have no update check. On Android you can turn it off in Settings, About, Check for updates when the app opens. On the Mac download the check is automatic and always asks before installing; it has no off switch in StreamSetu's Settings.
- **Apple iCloud (optional, off by default).** If you turn on iCloud sync, your library data and settings, and a record of when you watched what (the figures behind Statistics), are stored in your own private iCloud account, and logins can travel through iCloud Keychain. We cannot see them. You can turn sync off at any time and delete the data from Settings > Apple Account > iCloud.
- **The Movie Database, TMDB (on by default, can be turned off).** Unless you turn it off in Settings, StreamSetu sends the name and year of a title to TMDB to fetch artwork, summaries, cast and trailers, a person's name for the cast screen, and asks for the public weekly trending list. It uses a key built into the app, or your own free TMDB key if you add one. TMDB and its image servers (image.tmdb.org) see your internet address, as any web service does. Nothing else about you, your device, your viewing history, your library lists, your folder names or your profile is sent, and nothing from a locked, hidden or kids-restricted folder is sent; details saved for such a title are removed when you lock it. Saved details are removed after 180 days. You can turn artwork matching and weekly trending off in Settings (Artwork and Details), and no title is sent then.
- **iptv-org public channel database (on by default, can be turned off).** To fill in missing channel logos, genres and countries, StreamSetu downloads whole public files (channel list, feeds, logos and a block list) from iptv-org.github.io about once a day and matches them on your device. Nothing from your library or playlist is sent; the requests are plain file downloads, so iptv-org's host (GitHub Pages, run by GitHub) sees your internet address. Locked, hidden, adult and kids channels are never touched. Turn it off in Settings, Guide sources, Fill missing logos, genres and countries.
- **More artwork and episode details: fanart.tv, TheTVDB and TVmaze.** To find extra artwork (logos, backgrounds, posters) and episode and schedule details, StreamSetu asks these services about a title you open or see, using only its public numeric id (a TMDB, TheTVDB or IMDb number) or a country code, plus an access key in a request header. No title text, viewing history, library list, folder name, profile data, device identifier or account detail is sent, and nothing from a locked, hidden or kids-restricted folder is ever requested. The services and their image servers (assets.fanart.tv, artworks.thetvdb.com, static.tvmaze.com) see your internet address, as any web service does. fanart.tv and TVmaze are on by default; TheTVDB is also on by default using the app's key (or your own key if you add one); it receives only titles, years and public ids, and sees your internet address. What is saved on your device is removed after 180 days for fanart.tv art, 7 days for TheTVDB and a short time for TVmaze schedules, and you can turn each service off in Settings (Artwork and Details), after which it receives nothing. If a service is off or does not answer, the app quietly uses TMDB artwork and your playlist's own information.
- **OpenSubtitles (optional).** If you add your own OpenSubtitles key, account name and password and ask for subtitles in the player, StreamSetu sends the title, its year and a language code to opensubtitles.com to find subtitles, and your account name and password to sign in so a file can be downloaded. They are kept in the Keychain (on Android, in encrypted storage) and are sent nowhere else.
- **Debrid services: Real-Debrid, AllDebrid and Premiumize (optional, off until you add your own account token).** If you paste an API token and ask to play a link from a file host, StreamSetu sends that link and your token to the service you chose (api.real-debrid.com, api.alldebrid.com or www.premiumize.me) to turn it into a direct address that plays. On the Mac download a magnet link can be sent in the same way; the iPhone and iPad apps take links only. The token is kept in the Keychain (on Android, in encrypted storage) and is sent only to that service. The service sees your internet address, your account and the link, and its own policy applies. Remove the token in Settings, Library, Debrid, and nothing more is sent.
- **Trakt (optional).** If you connect your own Trakt app, StreamSetu sends when you start and stop watching a title (its name, year and how far you got) to trakt.tv, under your Trakt account, and reads your Trakt watchlist when you import it. Nothing is sent while you watch privately.
- **An online AI for the assistant (optional, off until you add a key).** If you add a key for Google Gemini, Groq, NVIDIA, OpenRouter, Anthropic Claude, OpenAI or Ollama Cloud and use the assistant, StreamSetu sends that provider your messages and the conversation so far. While "let the assistant see the screen" is on, it also sends the name of the screen you are on, the title you have open, your search text, what is playing (never while you watch privately) and a few library counts, and whatever the assistant asks to look up on your device (library titles, guide entries, playback figures). Apple Intelligence runs on the device and sends nothing; an Ollama address on your own network stays on it. What the provider does with it is governed by that provider.
- **Wikipedia.** When the assistant looks up a film, series or person, the topic is sent to Wikipedia (wikipedia.org, and its image host wikimedia.org). It happens only when you use the assistant.
- **Apple's iTunes catalogue.** When you open a movie or series page, the title and year may be sent to Apple's public iTunes Search service (itunes.apple.com) to find an official trailer clip. In Podcasts on the Mac, the words you search for are sent to the same service to find shows, and the two-letter region of your Mac (for example "us") is sent to Apple's public podcast charts (rss.marketingtools.apple.com) to show the popular shows for your country; the feeds you follow are then read from the addresses their publishers give.
- **YouTube.** If you tap Watch Trailer and the trailer is a YouTube video, the video is shown by YouTube's own player, which can set its own cookies and is covered by Google's privacy policy.
- **Streaming services shown inside the Mac app (Mac only, you choose to open each one).** The Mac app can show the website of an outside streaming service in a web view, with your own account on that service: Netflix, Prime Video, Disney+, Hulu, HBO Max, Peacock, Paramount+, Apple TV, YouTube, Crunchyroll, Spotify and JioHotstar. Nothing loads until you open that service. While a service is open your Mac talks to that service's servers directly, and the service sees your internet address, your sign-in and what you do on its site; its own policy applies. StreamSetu does not see or store your password, does not read your credentials or the page (except for the optional YouTube and Spotify page options described below), and sends nothing from your library to the service. Each service's cookies and saved site data are kept by the system web engine in a separate store on your Mac that only that service uses, so the services cannot see each other's data and you stay signed in between launches. The Clear Website Data command for a service (in its menu and in Settings, Privacy) removes that service's data only, after you confirm, and signs you out of it. For JioHotstar, StreamSetu keeps a fixed region preference (India, English) that is shown in Settings and never sent to the service; StreamSetu does not look up your location, change it, hide it or route your connection elsewhere, and the service decides where it is offered. Whether a service plays video inside StreamSetu is up to the service and your Mac, and some may not work in this view. Netflix pages can also be opened in your default browser; for the other services, links to other websites are blocked with a notice. Downloads and camera and microphone requests from the sites are refused, and the app asks for no location permission, so the sites get none. The YouTube service (Mac only) also has two optional page options, both off until you turn them on in Settings, Privacy, Streaming Services or in the one-time card shown the first time you open YouTube, and each asks you to confirm first. Ad skipping runs a small script inside the YouTube page that removes ad data and hides ad boxes; it sends nothing anywhere and cannot be used on any other service. Sponsor skipping asks SponsorBlock (sponsor.ajay.app), a community service, which parts of a video people marked as sponsor reads: it sends only the first 4 characters of a hash (fingerprint) of the video's identifier, over HTTPS, with no cookies, account, sign-in or device identifier. The service cannot tell which video it is, though it sees your internet address as any web service does. The answer is kept in memory only. Neither option is ever active in a kids profile or while the app is locked, and turning one off stops it at the next page load. The Spotify service (Mac only) has two optional page options of its own, both off until you turn them on in Settings, Privacy, Streaming Services or in the one-time card shown the first time you open Spotify, and each asks you to confirm first. Hiding ads and upgrade prompts tells the web view to hide those parts of the page and not to contact Spotify's ad servers; muting audio ads runs a small script that mutes the page's sound only while the player shows that an ad is playing. Neither reads your account or cookies, and both send nothing anywhere. Spotify's terms prohibit blocking or circumventing its ads, so using them can lead to Spotify suspending or terminating your account; they are best effort and can stop working at any time, Premium accounts see no ads, and you choose them at your own risk. They are never active in a kids profile or while the app is locked, and they apply to Spotify only. Signing in with Google, where a service offers it, opens Google's own sign-in page or window inside the service's web view; you type your Google password there, StreamSetu never sees or stores it, and the sign-in is kept in that service's own store like any other. The iPhone and iPad apps have none of this.
- **Apple.** The App Store, TestFlight and iCloud are Apple services under Apple's privacy policy.

- **A DNS provider, only if you turn on App DNS (Settings, Network).** It is off by default; turn it off again in the same place. When on, StreamSetu looks up the names of the servers it connects to through the DNS provider you pick (Cloudflare, Google, Quad9, AdGuard, Control D (freedns.controld.com) or one of your own), over an encrypted connection and inside StreamSetu only. That provider can see those host names, and may be able to tell which IPTV or CDN service you use from them. It does not receive your playlist passwords, stream addresses beyond the host name, or your viewing history.

## What is sent to third-party services

Only to the metadata services (TMDB, fanart.tv, TheTVDB, TVmaze, OpenSubtitles, iTunes, Wikipedia): the title and year, or a public id such as a TMDB, TheTVDB or IMDb number, or a language or country code. Never your viewing history, library lists, folder names or profile data. Nothing from a locked, hidden or kids-restricted folder is ever sent. Every service you contact also sees your internet address, as any web service does. No request carries an advertising identifier, and nothing is sent to the developer except the optional Mac playback log described above.

## Credits

- This product uses the TMDB API but is not endorsed or certified by TMDB. Artwork and data from The Movie Database (themoviedb.org), shown with the TMDB logo in Settings, under Credits.
- Artwork from fanart.tv (https://fanart.tv).
- Metadata provided by TheTVDB.com (https://thetvdb.com).
- Episode and schedule data from TVmaze (https://www.tvmaze.com), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0.
- The Mac app updates itself with Sparkle (MIT licence). Video playback uses libmpv and FFmpeg. The full list is under Open-Source Licenses.

## Security

Credentials are stored in the Keychain (on Android, in encrypted storage). An access token for a media server is sent only to that server, and is removed from a request if a redirect sends it to another one. The phone-assisted setup on a TV sends what you type over your home network without encryption, so use it on your own Wi-Fi only. Connections use TLS with certificate validation, and sensitive addresses and tokens are removed from logs. The optional App Lock uses Face ID, Touch ID or your device passcode.

## Your choices

- In the Mac app, turn Share playback logs automatically off at any time in Settings → Send Feedback.
- Turn off artwork matching, trending, fanart.tv, TVmaze, TheTVDB and the iptv-org database in Settings (Artwork and Details; Guide sources), and App DNS in Settings, Network.
- Do not add a debrid token, Trakt, OpenSubtitles or assistant key, and none of those services is contacted. Remove one to stop it.
- Remove a playlist, and its logins and data are removed from the device.
- Delete the app and its data is removed from the device.
- Turn off iCloud sync and delete the iCloud data from your Apple Account settings.

## Children

StreamSetu is a general-purpose player and contains no content of its own. Parental controls let you hide folders behind a PIN.

## Changes

If this policy changes, the new version appears in the app and on the website with a new date.

## Contact

Questions: contact@lokeshreddy.dev

